# Liontrust Asset Management PLC
@@ Executive Remuneration Review, FY2021–FY2026 · LIO LN · Year-by-year analysis and UK asset management benchmarking · 30 August 2026

## Headline findings

| Metric | Position |
|---|---|
| **CEO single figure, FY2021** | ~£6.2–6.6m on a £348k salary — variable pay of roughly **17–18x** base |
| **CEO single figure, FY2026** | **~£1.03m** on a ~£584k salary — variable pay of **~0.8x** base |
| **Six-year change in CEO pay** | **-84%** |
| **Six-year change in share price** | **-88%** (2,560p peak, Sep-21, to ~306.5p) |
| **Six-year change in base salary** | **+68%** (£348k to ~£584k) — the only component that rose |
| **Policy maximum variable (CEO)** | **800% of salary** (450% bonus + 350% LTIP) |
| **Largest shareholder revolt** | **45.9% against** the remuneration policy, GM of 16 Feb 2022 |
| **Most recent remuneration vote** | **72.57% in favour** (27.43% against), AGM of 18 Sep 2025 |
| **CEO ownership** | **~1.65%** — against Ashmore's founder-CEO at 32.19% |
| **Outstanding exec LTIP options (2025+2026 grants)** | **~1,575,000 shares = 2.5% of issued capital** |

> **The one-paragraph verdict.** Liontrust's remuneration has been substantially fixed. The pathology of 2021–22 — a bonus pool funded as a direct share of Adjusted Profit Before Tax, producing a £6.6m package on a £348k salary, followed by a 58% salary increase proposed into a collapsing share price — was addressed by the 2022 policy reset, which hard-capped the bonus and severed the profit link. Since then the Remuneration Committee has behaved creditably: bonuses modest and fully deferred into shares, LTIP grants scaled back below policy maxima in two consecutive years, and CEO pay down 84%. **But three problems remain.** The policy maxima are extraordinary for a £193m company; base salary is the one line that has only ever risen, and it rose 68% while shareholders lost 88%; and nil-price LTIP options granted at trough prices are quietly rebuilding a very large leveraged claim on any recovery — two years of grants alone equal 2.5% of the share capital and offset 42% of the £10m buyback the company has just completed.

[PAGEBREAK]

## 1 · Methodology and disclosure limitations

**What this report does.** It reconstructs the remuneration of Liontrust's two executive directors — John Ions (CEO) and Vinay Abrol (CFO/COO) — for the six financial years ended 31 March 2021 to 31 March 2026, decomposes it into salary, annual bonus, deferred share awards, LTIP and other elements, tests it against company performance, and benchmarks both the *structure* and the *quantum* against UK-listed asset management practice.

**Sources.** Company RNS announcements (including the 6 August 2026 LTIP grant notification and Deferred Bonus and Variable Allocation Plan awards), general meeting and AGM voting results, the 2022 Directors' Remuneration Policy as reported, and contemporaneous coverage in Citywire, Portfolio Adviser, City A.M., LexisNexis and Investment Week, together with ownership and compensation analytics.

> **Disclosure caveat — read this before relying on any figure.** Direct access to liontrust.com, Investegate and the primary Annual Report PDFs was blocked by this environment's network egress proxy. Single-figure totals are therefore taken from secondary sources and analytics providers rather than read from the audited Directors' Remuneration Report. They are reliable as to **order of magnitude, direction and structure**, but should be verified line-by-line against the 2026 Annual Report before use in a voting decision, an engagement letter or a public campaign. Figures explicitly marked **(derived)** are the analyst's own calculation from disclosed data, with the arithmetic shown so it can be checked. Where sources conflict — notably the FY2021 CEO total, reported as both £6.2m and £6.6m — both are given.

**A note on the FY2021 discrepancy.** Citywire reported £6.2m; compensation analytics report £6.6m. The gap is most likely definitional (treatment of pension, benefits, all-employee share plans or the valuation date of share awards). We use "~£6.2–6.6m" throughout and do not build conclusions on the difference.

## 2 · The remuneration architecture, and how it changed

### 2.1 The old regime (to FY2022): a profit-share partnership inside a listed company

Until the 2022 reset, the defining feature of Liontrust's executive pay was that **the annual bonus was funded by, and directly linked to, Adjusted Profit Before Tax**. In substance this was a partnership profit-share bolted onto a listed-company structure. Its properties were:

- **Uncapped in practice.** Bonus scaled with profit, and profit scaled with AuMA, and AuMA scaled with markets. In a rising market the executives were long a levered call on the beta of global equities.
- **Base salary was almost irrelevant.** At £348k against a £6.6m total, salary was 5% of the package. The Committee later used precisely this fact — that "salary levels have only had one increase of 5% since 2015" — to justify the 2022 increases.
- **Weak line of sight to shareholder outcomes.** Adjusted PBT excludes the amortisation and impairment of exactly the acquired intangibles (Neptune, Architas, Majedie) that the executives were deploying shareholder capital to buy. In FY2024 the group reported adjusted PBT of £67.4m against a **statutory loss before tax of £0.6m** — a £68m gap. The incentive metric and the shareholder's return had come apart entirely.

### 2.2 The 2022 reset

Following the February 2022 revolt and a summer of consultation, the policy was amended. The two changes that matter:

1. **A hard cap on the annual bonus.**
2. **Removal of the direct link to, and funding from, Adjusted Profit Before Tax.**

The resulting architecture is conventional in shape but unusually generous in scale:

| Component | CEO (John Ions) | CFO (Vinay Abrol) | Comment |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Base salary** | ~£584k (FY2024–FY2026) | ~£446k **(derived)** | Raised 58% and 28% respectively under the contested 2022 policy |
| **Annual bonus — policy maximum** | **450% of salary** | **350% of salary** | Hard-capped from 2022; in practice paid far below cap |
| **Bonus deferral** | **100% deferred into shares**, vesting over three years, via the Deferred Bonus and Variable Allocation Plan (DBVAP) | Same | A genuinely strong feature — see §4.3 |
| **LTIP — policy maximum** | **350% of salary** | **250% of salary** | Nil-price options |
| **LTIP performance conditions** | Total shareholder return, adjusted diluted EPS, and investment performance; three-year period; vesting from **10% at threshold to 100% at maximum** | Same | Three metrics, one of which (investment performance) is genuinely industry-appropriate |
| **Total variable at policy maximum** | **800% of salary (~£4.7m)** | **600% of salary (~£2.7m)** | The central governance question in this report |
| **Total package at policy maximum** | **~£5.3m** | **~£3.1m** | Against a £193m market capitalisation |

~ CFO salary and the implied LTIP grant price are derived in §4.4. Policy maxima are as reported at the time of the 2022 policy.

> **Frame the maxima properly.** A theoretical maximum CEO package of ~£5.3m equates to **2.7% of Liontrust's entire market capitalisation** and roughly **17% of FY2026 adjusted profit before tax**, in a single year, for one individual. Schroders — 40 times the AUM — paid its CEO ~£1.81m. The maxima have never been paid and, on current performance, cannot be. But a policy ceiling is a statement of intent, and this one was set at the top of the cycle and has never been revisited.

