~ The CFO's salary is derived in §4.4 from the disclosed August 2026 LTIP grant. Historic CFO single figures were not obtainable from the sources available; they should be read from the Annual Report remuneration tables.

### 3.3 What the shape of that table tells you

- **The collapse is real and it is large.** An 84% reduction in CEO pay over six years is at the severe end of anything seen in UK asset management. Pay has not been sticky here.
- **But the collapse is mechanical, not discretionary.** It follows almost entirely from the removal of the profit-linked bonus pool in 2022 and from profits falling. It is the *policy change* and the *earnings decline* doing the work, not a Committee choosing restraint. That distinction matters when assessing what happens if profits recover.
- **Salary went the other way.** Base salary is the only component that has risen — by 68% — across a period in which shareholders lost 88%, AuMA fell 42% and the dividend was cut 74%. The 2023 step from £348k to £550k is the single most contested decision in the company's governance history.
- **Variable pay has become almost vestigial.** At ~0.8x salary against a policy maximum of 8.0x, the executives are currently earning roughly **10% of what the policy permits**. That is either admirable restraint or a coiled spring, depending on your view of the recovery.

