## 4 · Component analysis

### 4.1 Base salary

| Effective from | CEO salary | Change | CFO salary | Change | Context |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 2015–2022 | £348k | one 5% rise in seven years | ~£290k (est.) | broadly flat | Deliberately low; the package was bonus-led |
| **1 Apr 2022** | **£550k** | **+58%** | ~£371k (est.) | **+28%** | Proposed Feb 2022; **ISS and Glass Lewis both recommended shareholders vote against** |
| FY2024 | **£584k** | +6% | ~£400k (est.) | — | |
| FY2025–FY2026 | £584k | **flat** | ~£446k **(derived)** | — | Frozen through the worst of the decline |

**Assessment.** The Committee's stated rationale — that salaries had been held artificially low for seven years because the package was bonus-led — is factually correct and, in isolation, defensible. The problem was **timing and calibration**. A 58% increase was proposed in February 2022, five months after the all-time high and as the de-rating was already under way, and it was paired with variable maxima that were themselves being raised to FTSE 250 levels. Shareholders were being asked to accept a permanent uplift in fixed pay *and* a generous new variable framework in the same resolution. They came within 4.1 percentage points of rejecting it.

To the Committee's credit, salary has been **frozen for three consecutive years** since FY2024. That is the correct response and should be acknowledged.

### 4.2 Annual bonus

| Year | Structure | Outcome |
|---|---|---|
| FY2021–FY2022 | Funded as a direct share of **Adjusted Profit Before Tax**; effectively uncapped | ~£5.7–6.3m implied for the CEO |
| FY2023 | Transitional; hard cap introduced, PBT link removed | ~£1.5m implied variable, down ~75% |
| FY2024–FY2026 | Hard cap at **450% of salary** (CEO); discretionary assessment against financial, strategic and personal measures | **Modest** — the Committee's own characterisation. Implied variable of ~£0.4–0.45m, i.e. roughly **10% of the cap** |

**Assessment.** The removal of the PBT funding link is the single most important governance improvement of the period. The current bonus is being paid at approximately a tenth of the permitted maximum, which is consistent with the Committee's stated reasoning of "ongoing net outflows, a material share price decline, and a challenging shareholder experience."

**The residual concern is the cap itself.** A 450%-of-salary bonus maximum is at the top of the UK asset management range for a company of this size. It is not being used, but it remains available the moment profits recover — and profits are highly geared to markets, which is precisely the exposure the 2022 reset was supposed to break.

### 4.3 Deferred bonus — the Deferred Bonus and Variable Allocation Plan (DBVAP)

**Both executives have deferred their entire annual bonuses into nil-price share options vesting over three years.** This is the strongest single feature of the current arrangement and deserves explicit credit:

- It converts cash compensation into a three-year equity exposure at the *current* depressed price, aligning the executives with the recovery rather than the reported profit.
- It removes the "bank the bonus and leave" optionality.
- It is materially better practice than the typical UK asset management structure of 50–60% deferral.

**The caveat.** Deferring 100% of a bonus that is only ~0.8x salary is a much smaller act of alignment than deferring 100% of a bonus that is 4x salary. The policy should be tested when the bonus recovers, not when it is small.

### 4.4 The Long Term Incentive Plan

| Grant | Date | CEO shares | CEO % of salary | CFO shares | CFO % of salary | Implied grant price | Implied CEO grant value |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **2025 grant** | Aug 2025 | **452,962 (derived)** | **297.5%** (85% of the 350% maximum) | **247,038 (derived)** | ~213% | ~383p **(derived)** | **~£1.74m** |
| **2026 grant** | **6 Aug 2026** | **566,203** | **284%** | **308,797** | **203%** | ~293p **(derived)** | **~£1.66m** |
| Change | | **+25% (capped)** | **-13.5pts** | **+25% (capped)** | **-10pts** | **-24%** | **-4.6%** |

**How the derivation works.** The 2026 grant is disclosed as 566,203 shares at 284% of salary. If salary is ~£584k, the grant value is £1.659m, implying a grant price of ~293p — consistent with the Liontrust share price in early August 2026. Applying the same price to the CFO's 308,797 shares gives £905k, which at 203% of salary implies a CFO salary of ~£446k. The 2026 award was disclosed as capped at a **25% increase in share numbers versus last year**, so the 2025 grants were 566,203 ÷ 1.25 = 452,962 and 308,797 ÷ 1.25 = 247,038 shares. At the disclosed 297.5% of salary, the 2025 CEO grant price implies ~383p.

**Performance conditions (2026 grant):** total shareholder return, adjusted diluted earnings per share, and investment performance, measured over three years from 1 April 2026 to 31 March 2029, vesting from 10% at threshold to 100% at maximum.

#### The mechanic shareholders should understand

> **Nil-price options granted at a depressed share price convert a falling share price into a rising share count.** Holding the award constant as a percentage of salary requires *more* shares as the price falls. The Committee recognised this and capped the increase at 25% — a genuinely responsible act that reduced the CEO's award from a policy-permitted 350% of salary to 284%. **But the cap limited the rate of increase, not the direction.** The share count still rose 25% in a year in which the shares fell ~24%.

**The cumulative effect is material.** The 2025 and 2026 grants alone total approximately **1,575,000 shares** — about **2.5% of the 62.97m shares in issue**. For context, the £10m buyback completed in June 2026 retired 3,764,532 shares. **Two years of executive LTIP grants therefore offset roughly 42% of the entire buyback programme.** Earlier grants and the DBVAP awards sit on top of that.

| Share price scenario | Value of the 2025+2026 LTIP grants if fully vested | Note |
|---|---|---|
| 306.5p (current) | **~£4.83m** | |
| 450p | **~£7.09m** | |
| 600p | **~£9.45m** | ~4.9% of today's market capitalisation |

~ Illustrative gross values assuming 100% vesting, which requires maximum performance against all three conditions; threshold vesting is 10%. Actual outcomes will be materially lower unless TSR, adjusted EPS and investment performance all reach stretch targets. The Committee has stated that recent LTIP vesting outcomes have been **low**.

### 4.5 Pension, benefits and all-employee arrangements

Detail is thin in the sources available, but the observable facts are:
- **Pension and benefits are a small proportion of the package** — the FY2026 CEO split of roughly 57% salary / 43% variable leaves limited room for material pension provision, consistent with UK best practice of aligning executive pension to the workforce rate.
- **Share Incentive Plan (SIP)** — both executives participate in the all-employee SIP, acquiring shares alongside staff.
- **Dividend Reinvestment Plan (DRIP)** — both reinvested their August 2026 dividend, each acquiring 1,157 shares at £3.05. Small in absolute terms, but the correct signal.

