## 5 · Pay for performance: the alignment test

### 5.1 Pay against the things shareholders actually own

| Financial year | CEO total pay | AuMA (year-end) | Gross profit | Adjusted PBT | Statutory PBT | Adj. diluted EPS | DPS | Share price context |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **FY2021** | **~£6.2–6.6m** | £30.9bn | n/d | n/d | n/d | n/d | 47p (est.) | Peak 2,560p reached Sep-21 |
| **FY2022** | **~£6.0m** | **£33.5bn** | n/d | n/d | £79.3m | n/d | 72p | De-rating begins |
| **FY2023** | **~£2.0m** | £31.4bn | £229.8m | £87.1m | £49.3m | 109.8p | 72p | GAM bid launched May-23 |
| **FY2024** | **~£1.0m** | £27.8bn | £186.1m | £67.4m | **-£0.6m** | 79.2p | 72p | Outflows £6.1bn |
| **FY2025** | **~£1.0m** | £22.6bn | £157.7m | £48.3m | £22.3m | 56.8p | 72p (**127% payout**) | Successive 52-week lows |
| **FY2026** | **~£1.03m** | £19.6bn | £123.0m | £30.5m | £14.4m | 36.7p | **19p** | Trough, then recovery |
| **Six-year change** | **-84%** | **-37%** | **-46%** (FY23 base) | **-65%** (FY23 base) | — | **-67%** (FY23 base) | **-74%** | **-88%** |

### 5.2 The alignment verdict

**Directionally aligned; historically not.**

- **FY2021–FY2022 — badly misaligned.** The CEO earned ~£12.2–12.6m across two years in which the share price peaked and then began an 88% decline, and in which the company committed up to £120m to the Majedie acquisition at the top of the UK equity cycle. Pay was calibrated to Adjusted PBT, which was itself calibrated to markets. Shareholders paid for beta and called it performance.
- **FY2023 — the crossover.** Pay fell 67% as the policy reset took effect. This is the year the alignment problem was structurally fixed.
- **FY2024–FY2026 — aligned, and arguably over-corrected.** CEO pay has been broadly flat at ~£1.0m for three years while adjusted PBT fell 55% and adjusted EPS fell 54%. Pay is now *less* variable than the underlying business — the opposite of the 2021 problem, and a legitimate retention concern if the recovery materialises.

> **The uncomfortable arithmetic.** Cumulative CEO remuneration across the six years is approximately **£17.2–17.6m**. Over the same period the equity lost roughly **£1.4bn** of market value from its September 2021 peak. Total pay therefore represents about **1.2% of the value destroyed**. That is not, in itself, an argument that the pay was wrong — value destruction was driven overwhelmingly by market de-rating and outflows, not by remuneration — but it is the number an activist will use, and the Committee should be ready for it.

### 5.3 Pay versus the workforce

Group staff costs fell **20%** in FY2026 and headcount was reduced from **210** (November 2024, when the efficiency programme was announced) to **170** before the River Global acquisition — a **19% reduction**, in addition to the earlier removal of ~25 roles. Over the same period CEO pay rose **~3%** and base salary was held flat.

**Assessment:** the CEO's *total* pay was broadly flat while ~40 colleagues lost their jobs. That is a defensible outcome on the numbers — pay did not rise materially — but it is not a strong one. A Committee wanting to demonstrate genuine solidarity had the option of reducing salary, and did not take it.

