## 6 · The 2021–22 pay revolt: a case study

| Date | Event | Outcome |
|---|---|---|
| **Sep 2021** | Non-executive director **Sophia Tickell departs** in the wake of shareholder disquiet over executive remuneration | An early warning that was not heeded |
| **Jan–Feb 2022** | Company proposes a new Directors' Remuneration Policy and LTIP: **58% salary increase for the CEO**, 28% for the CFO, bonus maximum of 450%/350% of salary, LTIP maximum of 350%/250% | Described in the market as a move to "FTSE 250-level pay" |
| **Feb 2022** | **ISS and Glass Lewis both recommend voting against** | |
| **16 Feb 2022** | **General Meeting.** Remuneration policy passes with **54.1%** in favour; LTIP with **55.6%** | **45.9% against — the largest vote against a remuneration policy at any UK company in 2022 at that point.** The company said it was "disappointed", noting "two rounds of engagement and consultation" with its top 20 shareholders |
| **Summer 2022** | Consultation with investors and proxy advisers | Leads to amendments: **a hard cap on the annual bonus** and **removal of the direct link to and funding from Adjusted Profit Before Tax** |
| **22 Sep 2022** | **2022 AGM.** Remuneration *report* receives **46.5% against** | A second consecutive revolt — the amendments came too late to repair the 2022 report |
| FY2023 onward | Policy operates as amended; CEO pay falls 67%, then 50% | The reset works |
| **18 Sep 2025** | **2025 AGM.** Remuneration report passes with **72.57% in favour (27.43% against)** | Dissent halved from 2022, but still **above the 20% threshold** that places a company on the Investment Association Public Register |

**What the episode demonstrates.**

1. **Engagement is not consent.** The company consulted its top 20 shareholders twice and still lost 45.9% of the vote. Consultation that does not change the proposal is not consultation.
2. **Shareholder pressure worked.** The two structural fixes that matter — the bonus cap and the removal of the PBT link — exist because shareholders voted against, not because the board proposed them.
3. **The discount persists.** Four years on, more than a quarter of votes are still cast against the remuneration report. Governance risk premia established in a revolt take a very long time to clear.
4. **Disclosure remains a live grievance.** In 2025 the Board consulted 19 of its largest institutional shareholders and did not consult proxy advisers, "as they were supportive". Shareholders nonetheless raised concerns about **disclosure of LTIP targets**, and the Board undertook to enhance LTI disclosures and bonus methodology. That undertaking will be tested at the September 2026 AGM.

