## 8 · Group-wide compensation

| Metric | FY2025 | FY2026 | Change |
|---|---|---|---|
| Gross profit | £157.7m | £123.0m | **-22%** |
| Total administrative expenses | — | — | **-16%** |
| **Staff costs** | — | — | **-20%** |
| Other administrative costs | — | — | **-9%** |
| Headcount | ~210 (Nov-24) | **170** (pre-River Global); ~212 post-completion | **-19%** before the acquisition |
| Adjusted operating margin | 29.2% | **24.0%** | -520bps |
| **Estimated compensation ratio** (staff costs ÷ gross profit) | **~45–50%** | **~42–48%** | Broadly stable |

~ Liontrust's absolute staff cost figure was not obtainable from the sources available for this note; the compensation ratio above is **estimated** by the analyst from adjusted operating costs of ~£93.5m and a typical 55–65% staff share, and should be replaced with the audited employee costs note from the 2026 Annual Report.

**Assessment.** Staff costs fell faster than revenue (-20% versus -22% gross profit, roughly in line), which is why the operating margin fell only 520bp rather than the ~800bp a fully fixed cost base would have produced. A compensation ratio in the low-to-mid 40s is **at the disciplined end** of the UK active management range, which typically runs 45–55% and can exceed 60% at performance-fee-driven boutiques. **On the group compensation line, Liontrust is managed well.** The governance issue is confined to the executive directors.

## 9 · Peer benchmarking: structure

| Practice | Liontrust | UK asset management norm | Verdict |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Bonus funded from a profit pool** | Until FY2022 — **direct link to Adjusted PBT** | Common at partnerships (Polar Capital, Artemis, Baillie Gifford); **rare and poorly regarded at listed companies** post-2018 | **Was an outlier; now fixed** |
| **Annual bonus maximum** | **450% of salary (CEO)** | 150–250% typical for a listed mid-cap; up to ~300% at large asset managers with low salaries | **Outlier — top of range** |
| **LTIP maximum** | **350% of salary (CEO)** | 150–250% typical | **Outlier — top of range** |
| **Total variable maximum** | **800% of salary** | 300–500% typical | **Significant outlier** |
| **Bonus deferral** | **100% into shares over three years** | 40–60% typical | **Better than peers** |
| **LTIP performance metrics** | TSR, adjusted diluted EPS, **investment performance** | TSR and EPS standard; **investment performance is used by better-governed managers** (Jupiter, Schroders) and omitted by weaker ones | **Better than peers** |
| **Vesting range** | 10% threshold to 100% maximum | 20–25% threshold is the UK norm | **A 10% threshold is soft** — it pays something for near-miss performance |
| **Salary restraint** | Frozen three years (FY2024–FY2026) | Inflationary increases common | **Better than peers** |
| **Grant sizing discipline** | Scaled back to 85% of maximum (2025) and capped at +25% share count (2026) | Many committees hold grants flat as a % of salary regardless of price | **Genuinely good practice** |
| **Grants at depressed prices** | Share count +25% while the price fell ~24% | The industry-wide problem; few UK committees address it properly | **Mitigated but not solved** |
| **Combined CEO/CFO tenure** | 16 years and 30+ years | Unusually long | **A structural governance issue in its own right** |

> **The structural paradox.** On *process* — deferral, metric choice, restraint in grant sizing, salary freezes — Liontrust's Remuneration Committee is now operating at or above the UK asset management standard. On *quantum ceilings* — an 800%-of-salary variable maximum at a £193m company — it remains a clear outlier. The Committee is behaving better than its own policy permits, which is reassuring about the current Committee and unreassuring about the policy.

