|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Liontrust** | **LIO LN** | **John Ions** | **~£1.03m** | **£24.3bn** | **~£193m** | **~£42k** | **~0.53%** |
| Jupiter Fund Management | JUP LN | Matthew Beesley | ~£2.16m | ~£68.4bn | ~£0.8bn | ~£32k | ~0.27% |
| Premier Miton | PMI LN | Mike O'Shea | ~£923k | ~£9.0bn | ~£58m | **~£103k** | **~1.59%** |
| Ashmore Group | ASHM LN | **Mark Coombs (founder)** | **~£229k** | ~£40bn | ~£1.47bn | **~£6k** | **~0.016%** |
| Schroders | SDR LN | Richard Oldfield | ~£1.81m | ~£770bn | ~£9.9bn (takeout) | ~£2.4k | ~0.018% |
| Aberdeen Group | ABDN LN | Jason Windsor | ~£3.19m | ~£500bn+ | ~£3.5bn | ~£6.4k | ~0.09% |

~ Peer figures are the most recent available from public compensation disclosures and analytics as at August 2026 and are approximate; AUM and market capitalisations are indicative. Salary/variable splits reported for peers: Jupiter ~21.6% salary / 78.4% variable; Schroders ~20.7% / 79.3%; Aberdeen ~24.2% / 75.8%; Premier Miton ~37.9% / 62.1%; **Liontrust ~56.6% / 43.4%**.

### 10.1 Reading the table

- **On absolute quantum, Liontrust is not an outlier.** £1.03m for the CEO of a £24bn manager is unremarkable — below Jupiter, well below Schroders and Aberdeen, and above only Premier Miton, which is a third of Liontrust's size.
- **On pay per £bn of AUM, Liontrust is expensive** — ~£42k versus ~£32k at Jupiter and ~£6k at Ashmore — but this metric penalises sub-scale managers structurally and should not be over-weighted.
- **On pay as a percentage of market capitalisation, Liontrust screens poorly.** At 0.53%, the CEO's annual pay consumes roughly twice the proportion of shareholder value that Jupiter's does, and roughly 30 times Ashmore's or Schroders'. This is the metric that reflects the de-rating: pay has fallen 84%, but the market capitalisation has fallen further.
- **Liontrust's pay mix is now the most conservative in the peer group.** At ~57% fixed / ~43% variable, against 20–25% fixed at Jupiter, Schroders and Aberdeen, Liontrust's CEO is currently the *least* incentivised in the sector on a mix basis. Bulls will call that misalignment; the Committee will call it the natural consequence of paying modest bonuses in a bad year. **Both are right, and the tension resolves only when profits recover.**

### 10.2 The Ashmore comparison

The most instructive contrast in UK asset management is Ashmore. Mark Coombs founded the business, owns **32.19%** of it (worth ~£331m), and takes total annual remuneration of approximately **£229k**. His incentive is his shareholding. John Ions owns **~1.65%** (worth ~£2.7m) and takes ~£1.03m a year.

> **The alignment ratio.** Coombs' annual pay is worth about **0.07%** of his shareholding — he is, in economic terms, a pure owner. Ions' annual pay is worth about **38%** of his entire shareholding. Put differently: **a 38% rise in the Liontrust share price would deliver John Ions roughly the same economic benefit as one year's salary and bonus.** For Coombs, replicating one year's pay requires a 0.07% move. This is the structural reason to be sceptical that incumbent management will pursue a sale of the company: the recurring compensation stream is worth far more to them than the control premium on their equity.

[PAGEBREAK]

## 11 · Assessment: red flags and green flags

### 11.1 Green flags — what the Committee has done right

1. **Removed the Adjusted PBT bonus link (2022).** The most important governance fix of the period, and the reason pay fell 84%.
2. **Hard-capped the annual bonus.**
3. **100% bonus deferral into three-year shares.** Better than the UK norm.
4. **Investment performance as an LTIP metric.** Appropriate to the industry and not universal among peers.
5. **Salary frozen for three consecutive years.**
6. **LTIP grants scaled back in two consecutive years** — to 85% of maximum in 2025, and capped at a 25% share-count increase in 2026 — with explicit reference to outflows, share price decline and shareholder experience. Many committees would simply have held the percentage of salary constant.
7. **Group staff costs cut 20%**, faster than most peers, holding the compensation ratio in the low-to-mid 40s.
8. **Executives buy shares.** SIP participation and DRIP reinvestment at £3.05.

### 11.2 Red flags — what remains unresolved

1. **The policy maxima are indefensible at this size.** 800% of salary in total variable, implying a ~£5.3m theoretical CEO package — 2.7% of market capitalisation — at a company with £24bn of AUM and £30.5m of adjusted profit. The policy has not been revised since 2022 and is due for renewal.
2. **Base salary rose 68% while shareholders lost 88%.** The 2022 increase is permanent, compounds into bonus and LTIP maxima (both expressed as a percentage of salary), and has never been revisited.
3. **A 10% vesting threshold is soft.** UK practice is 20–25%. A 10% floor pays out for performance that materially misses target.
4. **Nil-price options at trough prices.** Two years of grants equal 2.5% of issued capital and offset 42% of the completed buyback. The 25% cap slowed this; it did not stop it. **There is no disclosed mechanism preventing windfall gains on a recovery driven by market beta rather than management action.**
5. **Adjusted diluted EPS remains an LTIP metric.** The same adjusted measure that produced £67.4m of "profit" against a statutory loss of £0.6m in FY2024. Any LTIP metric that excludes the amortisation and impairment of acquired intangibles rewards management for acquisitions regardless of whether they earn their cost of capital.
6. **Persistent shareholder dissent.** 45.9%, then 46.5%, and still 27.43% against in 2025 — four years of above-threshold opposition.
7. **LTIP target disclosure remains a stated shareholder grievance**, with an undertaking given in 2025 that has yet to be visibly delivered.
8. **Extreme tenure with modest ownership.** A 16-year CEO and a 30-year CFO holding ~3.3% between them, paid ~£1.76m a year combined. The pay stream, not the equity stake, is the dominant economic interest — which shapes the board's incentives on any approach for the company.

### 11.3 Overall

> **Grade: B- and improving, from a D+ in 2022.** The structural pathology has been fixed and the current Committee is exercising real restraint. But the restraint is discretionary, the policy that permits far more remains in force, and the executives are accumulating a large, leveraged, low-strike claim on a recovery that they have not yet delivered. **The correct shareholder posture is engagement on the policy ceiling and LTIP disclosure — not opposition to current outcomes, which are reasonable.**

## 12 · The 17 September 2026 AGM: what to watch

| Item | Why it matters | What would be a good outcome |
|---|---|---|
| **Remuneration report vote** | The trend line: 45.9%  to  46.5%  to  n/d  to  n/d  to  27.43% against | Below 20% would clear the Public Register for the first time since 2021 |
| **LTIP target disclosure** | The Board undertook in 2025 to enhance LTI disclosure and bonus methodology | Specific, quantified TSR, adjusted EPS and investment performance targets, disclosed prospectively |
| **Policy renewal timing** | The 2022 policy is approaching the end of its normal three-year cycle | A binding vote on a **reduced** variable maximum — 300%/250% would still be generous |
| **Vesting threshold** | 10% is soft against a 20–25% UK norm | Raise the threshold |
| **Windfall-gain provisions** | Grants made at ~293p and ~383p into a recovering market | An explicit Committee discretion to scale back vesting for beta-driven gains |
| **Senior Independent Director succession** | Rebecca Shelley is not standing for re-election | A successor with credible remuneration and asset management experience |
| **Activist engagement** | GAM's special situations funds hold **5.01%** and have called for a strategic review | Whether remuneration becomes a vector for that campaign |

## 13 · Fifteen questions for the Remuneration Committee Chair

*Ordered by information value.*

1. The 2022 policy permits total variable pay of 800% of salary for the CEO — a theoretical package of ~£5.3m, or 2.7% of market capitalisation. At what AuMA and profit level would the Committee regard that maximum as payable, and will it be reduced at the next policy renewal?
2. Base salary rose 68% between 2022 and 2024 while total shareholder return was approximately -85%. Bonus and LTIP maxima are both expressed as a percentage of salary. Has the Committee considered rebasing salary, and if not, why not?
3. The 2026 LTIP grant increased the CEO's share count by 25% while the share price fell ~24%. What explicit mechanism prevents a windfall gain if the shares recover on market beta rather than management delivery?
4. LTIP vesting runs from 10% at threshold. UK practice is 20–25%. What is the rationale for a threshold that pays for materially missing target?
5. Adjusted diluted EPS is an LTIP metric. In FY2024 adjusted PBT was £67.4m against a **statutory loss** of £0.6m. Why should an incentive metric exclude the amortisation and impairment of the acquired intangibles that management chose to buy?
6. In 2025 the Board undertook to enhance LTI target disclosure following shareholder concern. What specifically has changed in the 2026 report, and will prospective targets now be quantified?
7. The 2025 and 2026 LTIP grants total ~1,575,000 shares — 2.5% of issued capital, offsetting ~42% of the £10m buyback. How does the Committee reconcile returning capital via buyback while simultaneously issuing equity to executives?
8. Group headcount fell from 210 to 170 and staff costs fell 20% in FY2026, while CEO total pay rose ~3%. Did the Committee consider a salary reduction, and what was the reasoning against?
9. What proportion of the FY2026 annual bonus was awarded for financial performance, strategic delivery and personal objectives respectively, and what were the specific measures?
10. Both executives have deferred 100% of their bonuses into shares. Will that 100% deferral be maintained if the bonus recovers toward the 450% cap?
11. The CEO has served 16 years and the CFO over 30. What is the Committee's view on the interaction between extreme tenure, LTIP quantum, and the retention rationale that normally justifies large awards?
12. Executive shareholdings are ~1.65% and ~1.71%. Ashmore's founder-CEO holds 32% and is paid ~£229k. What in-post and post-cessation shareholding requirements apply, and are they sufficient?
13. Following the River Global acquisition, will the acquired investment teams sit inside the group remuneration framework, and what is the resulting effect on the compensation ratio?
14. Three consecutive years of shareholder opposition exceeded 20%. What specific change would the Committee make if the September 2026 vote again exceeds that threshold?
15. If an offer were made for the company, how would in-flight LTIP and DBVAP awards be treated, and has the Committee modelled the value to executives of a change of control against the value of continued independence?

[PAGEBREAK]

## 14 · Conclusion

Liontrust's executive remuneration divides cleanly into two eras. Until FY2022 the executives were paid as partners in a private profit-share, taking ~£6m a year on a £348k salary from a bonus pool funded directly out of Adjusted Profit Before Tax. That structure was an outlier among listed UK asset managers, it rewarded market beta rather than management skill, and shareholders came within four percentage points of voting it down in February 2022. The reset that followed — a hard bonus cap and the removal of the profit-funding link — is the single most consequential governance improvement in the company's recent history and drove an 84% reduction in CEO pay.

The current Committee is behaving well. Salary has been frozen for three years, bonuses are being paid at roughly a tenth of the permitted cap and deferred entirely into three-year shares, LTIP grants have been scaled back below policy maxima in two consecutive years with explicit reference to shareholder experience, and group staff costs have been cut 20% — faster than revenue. On process and restraint, Liontrust now compares favourably with its listed peers.

Three things still need fixing. The **policy ceiling** of 800% of salary is indefensible for a £193m company and is due for renewal. The **68% salary increase** granted into a collapsing share price is permanent, compounds through every variable maximum, and has never been revisited. And **nil-price options granted at trough prices** are building a large leveraged claim on a recovery — two years of grants alone equal 2.5% of the share capital and offset 42% of the buyback the company has just completed — with no disclosed protection against windfall gains driven by market beta.

> **The conclusion an owner should draw.** The remuneration outcomes of the last three years are reasonable and do not warrant opposition. The remuneration *policy* that permits several times those outcomes does warrant engagement, and the September 2026 AGM — with the Senior Independent Director departing, an activist above 5%, and the Board's own 2025 undertaking on LTIP disclosure falling due — is the moment to press it. **The relevant risk is not that management is overpaid today. It is that the framework allows them to be substantially overpaid the moment markets do the work.**

